---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Types of Slip & Fall Cases | slipfallcalculator.com';
const description = 'Common types of slip and fall cases: retail stores, restaurants, parking lots, sidewalks, and more.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Types of Slip &amp; Fall Cases</h1>

  <h2>Retail Store Falls</h2>
  <p>Spilled merchandise, wet floors, uneven floor transitions, and cluttered aisles. Stores have a duty to inspect and clean hazards regularly. Security camera footage is often key evidence.</p>

  <h2>Restaurant Falls</h2>
  <p>Wet floors near drink stations, grease spills in kitchens (for employees), and uneven flooring. High-traffic environments create frequent hazards.</p>

  <h2>Parking Lot &amp; Sidewalk Falls</h2>
  <p>Ice, snow, potholes, and cracked pavement. Property owners must reasonably maintain outdoor surfaces. Municipal sidewalk cases may involve government immunity issues and short notice deadlines.</p>

  <h2>Stairway Falls</h2>
  <p>Broken handrails, uneven steps, poor lighting, or missing non-slip treads. Building code violations can establish negligence per se.</p>

  <h2>Apartment &amp; Residential Property Falls</h2>
  <p>Landlords must maintain common areas in safe condition. Tenant injuries from defective stairs, broken locks, or slippery walkways often give rise to premises liability claims.</p>

  <h2>Workplace Falls</h2>
  <p>Workers' compensation typically covers employee injuries at work. Third-party tort claims may be available if the hazard was created by someone other than the employer (e.g., a contractor).</p>

  <h2>Government Property Falls</h2>
  <p>Falls on government-owned property (public sidewalks, courthouses, parks) involve sovereign immunity rules. Most states require a formal notice of claim filed within 60–180 days of injury.</p>
</BaseLayout>
